    ball.vy = ball.y < brick.y + brick.h / 2 ? -Math.abs(ball.vy) : Math.abs(ball.vy);
    ball.y = ball.y < brick.y + brick.h / 2 ? brick.y - ball.r : brick.y + brick.h + ball.r;
  }
```
For the top face hit:
`ball.x = 100, ball.r = 10, brick.x = 90, brick.w = 20`.
`ball.x + ball.r = 110, brick.x = 90`.
`ball.x - ball.r = 90, brick.x + brick.w = 110`.
`overlapX = min(110, 110) - max(90, 90) = 110 - 90 = 20`.

`ball.y = 80, ball.r = 10, brick.y = 90, brick.h = 20`.
`ball.y + ball.r = 90, brick.y = 90`.
`ball.y - ball.r = 70, brick.y + brick.h = 110`.
`overlapY = min(90, 110) - max(70, 90) = 90 - 90 = 0`.

Wait, `overlapY = 0`.
So `overlapY < overlapX` (0 < 20) is true.
`ball.y = 80`. `brick.y + brick.h / 2 = 90 + 10 = 100`.
`80 < 100` is true.
So `ball.vy = -Math.abs(100) = -100`.
`ball.y = 90 - 10 = 80`.

Wait, if the tests say `100 !== -100`, then `handleBrickCollision` returned `false` or didn't flip it.
Wait, I see: `if (!collides(ball, brick)) return false;`.

Does `collides(ball, brick)` return true for `overlapY = 0`?
```javascript
export function collides(ball, brick) {
  return (
    ball.x + ball.r > brick.x &&
    ball.x - ball.r < brick.x + brick.w &&
    ball.y + ball.r > brick.y &&
    ball.y - ball.r < brick.y + brick.h
  );
}
```
`ball.y + ball.r > brick.y` => `90 > 90` is FALSE.
So `collides` returns `false` when the ball is just touching the edge.

The tests have the ball at `y = 80, r = 10`, so `y + r = 90`. The brick is at `y = 90`.
So it's a perfect touch. `collides` requires strictly greater than.

I should change `collides` to use `>=` and `<=` to be more inclusive of the edge.

